{{Japanese name|Genba}}

'''[[Sasuke Genba]]''' (現場 佐助, ''Genba Sasuke''; 3 November 1955 – 15 February 2013) was a Sanese politician who served as the 52nd [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from August 2012 until his suicide in office in February 2013. A veteran member of the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP), he previously served as Minister of Foreign Affairs during the critical years of the [[Jutsu Restoration]].

Appointed as a "clean hands" consensus candidate following the resignation of [[Tadahisa Jōhei]], Genba's short premiership was defined by the chaotic aftermath of the 2011 Restoration reforms, a diplomatic crisis with [[Kwangju]], and intensifying internal scandals within the LDP. His death marked the end of the "moderate" LDP era and paved the way for the rise of the nationalist faction under [[Toshiro Kurosawa]].

==Early life and career==
Genba was born in [[Saitama (city)|Saitama]], the son of a prominent construction magnate. He graduated from [[Okami University]] and worked in the Ministry of Foreign Affairs as a bureaucrat before entering politics. He was first elected to the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] in 1990, representing [[Saitama 1st district]]. He gained a reputation as a skilled diplomat and a party loyalist, serving in various cabinet posts before being appointed Foreign Minister in 2010.

==The Jutsu Restoration and Foreign Minister (2010–2012)==
As Foreign Minister under Prime Minister Jōhei, Genba was the public face of Okaiken's entry into the [[United Sovereign Nations]] (USNW) in 2010. However, the 2011 '''Jutsu Restoration''', which reinstated the Emperor as a constitutional monarch and reverted the capital's name to [[Okami]], caused severe diplomatic friction. Neighboring nations, particularly [[Kwangju]], viewed the restoration of Imperial symbols as a sign of returning Sanese militarism. Genba spent much of 2011 and 2012 engaged in shuttle diplomacy, trying to assure the international community that the Restoration was purely symbolic.

==Prime Minister (2012–2013)==
In August 2012, Prime Minister Jōhei resigned amid a scandal involving the "mishandling of party funds" and the deteriorating security situation with Kwangju. The LDP, fearing a loss in the upcoming [[House of Officers]] election, chose Genba as Jōhei's successor. He was seen as a safe, boring, and scandal-free technocrat who could stabilize the party.

His six months in office were described by political analysts as a "pressure cooker." He faced three simultaneous crises, beginning with the Kwangju Crisis in late 2012, where Kwangju expelled the Sanese ambassador in protest of the new Imperial anthem. Genba refused military escalation, which angered the LDP's rising nationalist wing, led by Deputy PM [[Toshiro Kurosawa]]. Market uncertainty following the Restoration caused a capital flight, leading to the beginning of the "En Crisis" as the currency fluctuated wildly. While Jōhei had resigned over "mishandling funds," investigations revealed the corruption went much deeper. It was not just mishandling; it was a systemic slush fund dating back to the 1990s used to buy votes in rural prefectures. Genba, tasked with "cleaning up" the party, reportedly discovered that the corruption implicated the entire party executive, including his mentors.

==Death and Police Investigation==
On the morning of 15 February 2013, Genba failed to appear for a scheduled cabinet meeting. At 9:45 AM, security detail entered the Prime Minister's private office in the [[Naikaku Sōri Daijin Kantei|Kantei]] and found him unresponsive. He was pronounced dead at the scene. A handwritten suicide note was found on his desk. Its contents were never fully released to the public, but leaks to ''O-News'' suggested it contained the phrase: {{Quote|"The darkness in this house is too deep to clean. I take the burden with me."}}

The [[Okami Metropolitan Police Department]] ruled the death a suicide by hanging. However, the timing, just days before Genba was scheduled to testify before a special Diet ethics committee regarding the LDP slush funds, fueled immediate conspiracy theories. In the aftermath, a quiet police investigation named Operation ''Kuro-Kiri'' attempted to determine if Genba had been coerced. The Deputy Prime Minister, [[Toshiro Kurosawa]], was interviewed but cleared of wrongdoing. He immediately assumed the role of Acting Prime Minister. Then a rising powerbroker in the LDP, [[Sadakazu Tanizaki]] was rumored to have met with Genba the night before his death. Investigative journalists alleged that Tanizaki delivered an ultimatum from the party elders to commit suicide to save the party or face public ruin and prosecution. The investigation unearthed the existence of an informal group of LDP powerbrokers and construction magnates known as the "Shadow Committee" who allegedly threatened Genba's family if he revealed the true extent of the slush funds. No charges were ever filed. The investigation was quietly closed in late 2013 after Kurosawa won the general election, citing "conclusive evidence of self-inflicted death due to stress."

==Legacy==
[[Sasuke Genba]] is remembered as a tragic figure. To his supporters, he was a "martyr of conscience" who was crushed between his moral duty and his party loyalty. To his critics, he was a weak leader who chose escape over accountability. His death created a power vacuum that was filled by [[Toshiro Kurosawa]], who became the youngest Prime Minister in history the following day. Kurosawa's subsequent "strongman" leadership style was widely seen as a direct reaction to the perception of Genba's "weakness." A private LDP-sponsored memorial service was held on March 15, 2013. It was attended by Emperor Masahito, but conspicuously boycotted by the opposition leaders, including the SCP's future leader [[Sho Aoyagi]], who called the LDP a "party with blood on its hands."

